.TH MIKOSHI 8 "2026-11-07" "mikoshi 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mikoshi \- snapshot backups for Arch Linux, built on rsync
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mikoshi
.RB [ \-v ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mikoshi
backs up chosen parts of an Arch Linux system (system settings, dotfiles, app settings,
desktop and theme, package lists, boot and disk layout, home folders, browser profiles,
keys, or the whole system) to a folder, a drive or an NFS share.
.PP
Every run makes a
.I snapshot:
a normal folder holding a complete copy of the backed-up paths. Files that didn't change
since the previous snapshot are hard links to it, so each snapshot looks complete but only
changed files take extra space. Snapshots can be browsed with any file manager and restored
with plain
.BR cp (1)
or
.BR rsync (1)
if Mikoshi itself isn't available.
.PP
Configuration lives in
.IR /etc/mikoshi/config.toml ;
see
.BR mikoshi.conf (5).
The graphical app
.B mikoshi\-gui
does everything the commands below do.
.SH COMMANDS
Most commands need root. Read-only commands accept
.B \-\-json
for scripts.
.TP
.B status
Jobs, their last result and next scheduled run. Works without root.
.TP
.BI run " job"
Run a backup now and wait for it, with live progress on a terminal.
.B \-\-no\-prune
skips removing old snapshots afterwards.
.TP
.BI start " job"
Start a backup in the background (as a systemd unit), so it survives closing the terminal.
.TP
.BI cancel " job|key"
Stop a running backup, restore or prune. A cancelled backup keeps its partial copy and the
next run continues from there.
.TP
.BI list " job"
List the snapshots of a job.
.TP
.BI show " job snapshot"
Details of one snapshot: modules, paths, sizes, warnings.
.TP
.BI browse " job snapshot " [ path ]
List files inside a snapshot.
.B \-\-meta
lists the saved metadata instead (package lists, disk layout, service lists).
.TP
.BI restore " job snapshot path..."
Restore files or folders, given by their original absolute paths. Shows a preview and asks
before changing anything. Files it replaces are moved to
.I /var/lib/mikoshi/undo/<time>/
so a restore can be undone.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-to " dir"
Restore into another folder (full paths are recreated inside it).
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Also remove files that aren't in the backup, so folders match it exactly.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Only show what would change.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Allow restoring /usr, /boot and similar system folders over the running system.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Don't ask.
.RE
.TP
.BI prune " job"
Remove snapshots the job's retention rules no longer keep.
.B \-\-dry\-run
shows what would be kept and why.
.TP
.BI pin " job snapshot"
Keep a snapshot forever, optionally with
.BI \-\-note " text".
.B unpin
undoes it.
.TP
.BI delete " job snapshot"
Delete one snapshot.
.TP
.BI cleanup " path"
Remove this PC's Mikoshi folders at
.I path
(the folder, mounted drive or share holding the
.I mikoshi
folder) that no job uses any more. Folders without backups are removed; folders with
backups are only removed with
.BR \-\-delete\-backups .
Folders of jobs that still back up there are never touched. Changing destinations in the
app or with
.B config edit
does this automatically and asks before deleting backups.
.TP
.B migrate
Carry over an install of Lifeboat (Mikoshi's earlier name): config, history, logs and
schedules. Runs automatically after installing and before any command run as root.
.TP
.BI find\-backups " destination"
List the backups already stored at a destination, by PC: jobs, how many backups, newest.
.TP
.BI adopt " destination pc " [ job ...]
Use existing backups, for example after reinstalling: recreate the PC's jobs from the
settings saved with them and continue its backups on this computer. If
.I pc
differs from this PC's name, this PC takes that name. Imported jobs start turned off (so
backups of a fresh system can't push out the old ones before you restore), and commands
that run before or after backups are not imported.
.TP
.BI check " destination"
Test a destination: reachable, writable, filesystem, free space, and how file owners and
permissions will be stored.
.TP
.B drives
Drives (filesystem UUIDs) that can hold backups.
.TP
.BI exports " server"
NFS shares a server exports (needs nfs-utils).
.TP
.B modules
What can be backed up.
.TP
.B detect
What Mikoshi detects: firmware, filesystems, LUKS, LVM, bootloaders, desktops, AUR helper, users.
.TP
.B config show|edit|check|import|example|path
.B edit
opens the config in
.B $EDITOR
and validates it before saving.
.B import
takes TOML or JSON from a file or stdin.
.TP
.B schedule sync|show|cron
.B sync
writes and enables one systemd timer per scheduled job (also done automatically when the
config changes through Mikoshi).
.B cron
prints equivalent crontab lines for people who prefer cron.
.TP
.BI history " " [ job ]
Past runs.
.TP
.BI log " job " [ name ]
The log of the latest (or a named) run.
.B \-\-list
lists available logs.
.SH HOW BACKUPS ARE STORED
.nf
<destination>/mikoshi/<host>/
    host.json                    which computer this folder belongs to
    <job>/
        job.json                 the job's settings, to recreate it after a reinstall
        latest -> snapshots/<newest>
        snapshots/
            2026-11-07_02-30-00/
                files/           the backed-up paths, laid out from /
                meta/            package lists, disk layout, service lists...
                manifest.json    what was backed up, how, and the result
                pinned           present when pinned
            .in-progress/        an unfinished run (resumed next time)
.fi
.PP
On destinations that can't store Linux owners directly but do support extended attributes
(for example an NFS 4.2 share that maps root to nobody), owners and permissions are stored in
extended attributes ("fake-super" mode). Restore those with Mikoshi, or with
.B rsync \-\-fake\-super.
.SH WHICH COMPUTER OWNS A FOLDER
Each PC keeps its backups in a folder named after it (its host name, or the name set in the
app or with
.B hostname
in
.BR mikoshi.conf (5)).
.I host.json
records one-way fingerprints of the computers allowed to write there (from
.IR /etc/machine\-id ;
the ID itself is not stored). If a different computer with the same name tries to back up
to the same place, the backup is refused instead of mixing the two. After a reinstall, use
.B adopt
(or Find Backups in the app) to continue the old backups; it adds the new computer's
fingerprint. Renaming the PC moves its folder at every reachable destination.
.SH RESTORING WITHOUT MIKOSHI
Copy files straight out of
.IR snapshots/<time>/files/ .
To keep owners and permissions:
.PP
.nf
rsync \-aHAX \-\-numeric\-ids <snapshot>/files/./home/me/.config /
.fi
.PP
The
.I meta/packages/reinstall.sh
script reinstalls the packages that were installed at backup time.
.SH TRIGGERS
Besides schedules, a job can run before every pacman transaction (through
.IR /usr/share/libalpm/hooks/05\-mikoshi.hook ;
it never blocks pacman) and when its destination drive is plugged in (through a generated
udev rule). Both respect the job's minimum gap between runs.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mikoshi/config.toml
Configuration (world-readable).
.TP
.I /etc/mikoshi/secrets.toml
Secrets such as the ntfy token (root only).
.TP
.I /var/lib/mikoshi/history.jsonl
Run history.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mikoshi/undo/
Files replaced by restores.
.TP
.I /var/log/mikoshi/<job>/
Per-run logs.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/mikoshi\-<job>.timer
Generated timers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success (also with warnings), 1 failure, 2 usage error,
75 skipped (destination unavailable, already running), 130 cancelled.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo mikoshi config edit
sudo mikoshi run daily
sudo mikoshi list daily
sudo mikoshi restore daily 2026-11-07_02-30-00 /home/me/.config/kitty
sudo mikoshi restore \-\-to /tmp/old daily 2026-11-07_02-30-00 /etc
sudo mikoshi prune \-\-dry\-run daily
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mikoshi.conf (5),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR systemd.timer (5),
.BR alpm\-hooks (5)
